    "loaded": "available_on_demand",
    "content": "# Task decomposition\n\n## When to use\n\nUse this advisory method when there are dependent steps, several possible starting points, or a blocker that might only affect one part of the task. For straightforward work, act directly. The plan belongs to the current task and changes when the user steers or new evidence changes what matters.\n\n## Find the critical path\n\n1. Name the outcome and where the user's request ends. Separate what they asked you to do from downstream events someone else controls.\n2. Work backward from that outcome. Find the first unresolved dependency that can change which action is worth taking. That is usually a better first step than choosing a tool or reopening an old plan.\n3. If the missing dependency is information, **research first**. Look for the smallest evidence that changes the decision. Pick the browser, local reader, or another source only after you know what you need to learn. If you already have enough information, skip general research and take the next useful action.\n4. Keep independent work moving. No previous attempt, no existing tab, or incomplete evidence of fit may change how you start; none is by itself an obstacle to researching the current task. Distinguish a reversible research step from an externally consequential action or one constrained by a real deadline.\n5. Once you have enough for the next decision, act and check the relevant result. End that step, not the whole task. When discovery serves a delivery goal, assess each viable item as it appears and act once it qualifies; do not wait for a batch unless the user requested a comparison. A failed item closes that option only. Stop at the user’s requested boundary, not after the first useful finding. Revisit unfinished steps when new evidence or direction changes the critical path.\n\n## Leave a usable handoff\n\nWhen a dependent step will use a discovery, preserve the smallest set of concrete details that lets it start: a stable link or ID, the source and what it actually established, any material requirements or route already checked, and what remains unverified. Put those details in the current task's observed outcome or a task-owned working artifact it names. Cite actual receipts separately. A receipt ID or search-result title alone is not necessarily enough to locate or assess the item in a new turn. Carry only what the next step needs; don't copy credentials or sensitive inputs into the outline.\n\nIf a later step has names but no usable source, preserve the original observation; don't relabel it as finished verification or make it pending again. Focus the ready step and retrieve the missing details from fresh task-owned sources, or add a narrow recovery step if the outline needs one. Search with the identifiers already available. Open or reuse a tab owned by the current task as appropriate; neither a missing saved link nor a missing old tab is by itself a blocker. Don't reread unrelated inputs. If a bounded fresh check still leaves a genuine blocker, report what you checked, what could not be verified, and any independent progress. Recovering an input does not authorize replay of an uncertain external action.\n\n## Examples\n\n**Job search:** “Find three jobs for me.” First identify the working criteria and evidence you can get cheaply (the user’s stated priorities and résumé if available). Then research current listings on appropriate job boards or employer sites. Inspect enough of each posting to judge whether it belongs on the shortlist; don’t turn missing proof of fit into disqualification. Stop at three defensible options. No application was requested.\n\n**Application:** “Apply for this role.” Once the role is known, research what you need to decide or submit: the current posting, stated requirements, route, and essential application materials. An application has little practical value to the user until the employer responds, so don’t front-load interview-level employer research. Research in depth if you get an interview, including a screen. The application task ends at the requested application outcome; an employer response comes later and is not an application step.\n\n**Discovery to preparation:** The search turn records three role names, but the preparation turn needs live postings and application requirements. Before handing off a discovery that's meant to support preparation, open the promising postings and carry their canonical links or posting IDs, employer and title, observed requirements and application route, plus anything not yet checked. If the old handoff contains only names, use them to search the current employer site or job board in this task; verify the exact posting and route instead of treating missing links as a blocker or rereading the résumé. If the exact posting is unavailable after a bounded check, say so and move on to other candidates. Preparing materials does not mean submitting them.\n\n**Debugging:** “Fix the chat that stopped loading after install.” Start by locating the first failed load in the installed build. If the entry module loads but an imported file returns 404, compare the package contents with what the installed server actually serves. Fix the boundary that failed and verify there. Don’t rewrite the chat system or repeat a passing source-only check that never exercised the installed route."
